1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
These are earth-shaking fires.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
A special breed of firefighters snuffs them.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Specialists who blow out raging infernos with explosives.

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:21,000
And cap fountains of highly flammable oil or gas without blowing themselves up.

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, oil firefighting on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Welcome to disaster.

7
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:42,000
It doesn't get any worse than a raging oil or gas well fire.

8
00:00:42,001 --> 00:00:47,000
Choking, burning smoke shoots hundreds of feet into the air.

9
00:00:47,001 --> 00:00:51,000
Without protection, the heat can cook a person's skin several

10
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:54,000
yards from the blaze.

11
00:00:54,001 --> 00:00:56,500
from the blaze.

12
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:00,400
But once it's out, it's even more dangerous.

13
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:02,680
The specialists work to cap the blowing well

14
00:01:02,780 --> 00:01:07,120
under a shower of flammable crew.

15
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:10,220
Standing beside a blowing well is like placing your head next

16
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:13,980
to a jet engine, only louder.

17
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:18,000
They're so loud that you put cotton and Vaseline in your ears

18
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:20,320
and then you shove an ear plug in behind that.

19
00:01:20,321 --> 00:01:25,120
Only a handful of men in the entire world

20
00:01:25,220 --> 00:01:27,260
are qualified to put out a fire like this.

21
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:33,660
You got to be careful nobody makes a mistake.

22
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:35,840
It's not just jumping and do this and do that.

23
00:01:35,940 --> 00:01:39,680
There's a lot more to it than people think.

24
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:43,040
Every well fire presents unique problems,

25
00:01:43,140 --> 00:01:46,420
but the basics of the job are always the same.

26
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:50,320
Put out the fire and cap the blowing.

27
00:01:50,321 --> 00:01:51,320
well.

28
00:01:55,620 --> 00:01:58,420
To avoid being incinerated while doing their incredibly

29
00:01:58,520 --> 00:02:02,940
dangerous work, experts use specialized tools and methods

30
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:04,800
perfected over the last 70 years.

31
00:02:07,340 --> 00:02:12,040
First of all, you have to gain ready access, safe access for

32
00:02:12,140 --> 00:02:14,440
the people to do the work.

33
00:02:14,540 --> 00:02:18,040
If there was a drilling rig and it caught fire and it's burnt

34
00:02:18,041 --> 00:02:23,320
it means, first of all, the safe removal of all that debris

35
00:02:23,420 --> 00:02:26,620
from around the well.

36
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:29,760
Firefighters use bulldozers to drag away the twisted

37
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:31,160
wreckage of a burnt up rig.

38
00:02:34,020 --> 00:02:38,060
They also use an Athey wagon, an A-frame trailer with a long

39
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:42,760
boom and a hook on the end, to grab debris.

40
00:02:42,860 --> 00:02:45,140
So we have to take it away a piece at a time,

41
00:02:45,141 --> 00:02:49,340
a piece at a time, because if we do put it out, one hot piece

42
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:52,180
of metal could reignite the well.

43
00:02:52,280 --> 00:02:56,380
Giant pumps spray a deluge of water over men and equipment,

44
00:02:56,480 --> 00:03:01,080
protecting them from the intense heat.

45
00:03:01,180 --> 00:03:03,200
This is one of our bigger pumps in our inventory.

46
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:07,100
This is a 5,000 gallons per minute pump.

47
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:11,500
You've got the engine as your driver, the transmission,

48
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:14,500
and the pump itself.

49
00:03:14,501 --> 00:03:17,240
the only thing between these firefighters and a raging

50
00:03:17,340 --> 00:03:21,940
inferno is a 1 1⁄8 inch piece of tin.

51
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:23,940
Well, out of all the equipment we've got,

52
00:03:24,040 --> 00:03:26,240
from expensive to the cheapest and smallest thing,

53
00:03:26,340 --> 00:03:28,340
this is probably one of the best friends you could possibly

54
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:30,540
have when you're on a rig fire.

55
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:32,160
It's a typical corrugated tin.

56
00:03:32,260 --> 00:03:36,860
There's a lot of barns built out of it, same type material.

57
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:41,760
The shiny galvanized sheet metal reflects heat.

58
00:03:41,761 --> 00:03:45,060
Firefighters also use it to protect equipment.

59
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:47,560
As long as you've got water and you've got a piece of tin,

60
00:03:47,660 --> 00:03:51,260
you can stand up and basically touch the fire.

61
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:54,200
There are many ways a disaster like a blowing, burning well

62
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:56,200
could ignite.

63
00:03:56,300 --> 00:04:02,620
An errant spark, lightning, or the friction of blowing debris.

64
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:07,620
It can even happen before a well begins producing.

65
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:11,060
When sinking a well, drillers may have encountered greater

66
00:04:11,061 --> 00:04:15,560
pressure from the oil reservoir trapped underground, leading to

67
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:19,300
the failure of surface containment equipment.

68
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:23,600
When the flow of crude is uncontrolled, that's a blowout.

69
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:27,140
And at any time, that combustible oil or gas can

70
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:29,480
catch fire.

71
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:32,720
Traditionally, one of the most effective ways to snuff a fire

72
00:04:32,780 --> 00:04:38,420
has been to literally blow it out using explosives.

73
00:04:38,421 --> 00:04:41,420
It's simply robbing it of oxygen.

74
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:46,420
You place that charge just near the base of the flame,

75
00:04:46,520 --> 00:04:49,960
where the fluid coming from the well

76
00:04:50,060 --> 00:04:53,960
is just becoming oxygen rich enough to burn.

77
00:04:54,060 --> 00:04:56,880
And that's put in a so-called shot drum,

78
00:04:56,980 --> 00:05:01,780
which is nothing more than either a 55-gallon or a 35-gallon

79
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:02,580
barrel.

80
00:05:02,581 --> 00:05:07,380
barrel.

81
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:10,380
The technique of using explosives to blow out fires

82
00:05:10,480 --> 00:05:11,780
has been around since the 1920s.

83
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:20,540
Today, blowout specialists occasionally still use explosives,

84
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:24,040
but most prefer less destructive methods.

85
00:05:24,140 --> 00:05:27,680
The same water pumps firefighters use to cool themselves

86
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:30,080
put out the fires too.

87
00:05:30,081 --> 00:05:33,040
Today, when you go put 6,000, 8,000 gallons a minute,

88
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:35,780
you can extinguish just about anything with that kind of water

89
00:05:35,860 --> 00:05:39,160
supply in the right place.

90
00:05:39,220 --> 00:05:41,000
Takes a lot of the thrill out of it,

91
00:05:41,060 --> 00:05:46,060
but it's a little more practical, really.

92
00:05:46,120 --> 00:05:48,460
But the specialists also have to cap the well.

93
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:53,800
Sometimes this is done while it's burning,

94
00:05:53,880 --> 00:05:57,940
if there's poisonous gas, or it poses a larger environmental

95
00:05:57,941 --> 00:06:01,940
a mental hazard to put it out.

96
00:06:01,941 --> 00:06:04,680
Many times the team has to cut off a damaged wellhead

97
00:06:04,681 --> 00:06:06,580
before attaching a new control device.

98
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:15,240
Specialists often use a tool called an abrasive jet cutter

99
00:06:15,241 --> 00:06:18,140
to do this job.

100
00:06:18,141 --> 00:06:21,400
The tool uses high pressure water mixed with sand

101
00:06:21,401 --> 00:06:25,180
to sever the damaged wellhead.

102
00:06:25,181 --> 00:06:32,220
Next, a capping stack with several blowout preventers, or BOPs, is forced over the blowing

103
00:06:32,221 --> 00:06:35,860
well and secured to the pipe.

104
00:06:35,861 --> 00:06:45,540
BOPs are also used during drilling to control well pressure.

105
00:06:45,541 --> 00:06:50,540
uses hydraulic rams that close and seal off the hole.

106
00:06:54,540 --> 00:06:57,540
And when you actually get the pleasure of shutting the blowout

107
00:06:57,541 --> 00:06:59,540
preventers in, it's the quietest quiet in the world.

108
00:06:59,541 --> 00:07:04,540
It's a very good feeling. Clearly, it's a dangerous

109
00:07:04,541 --> 00:07:07,540
line of work, and it always has been.

110
00:07:10,540 --> 00:07:14,540
Thousands of people have died fighting these fires over the past

111
00:07:14,541 --> 00:07:20,940
past century and a half. In August 1859, oil

112
00:07:20,941 --> 00:07:24,520
speculator Edwin Drake drilled the first oil well in the United

113
00:07:24,521 --> 00:07:29,920
States. Two months later, it caught fire.

114
00:07:29,921 --> 00:07:32,520
Fire becomes a problem in the oil industry as soon as you

115
00:07:32,521 --> 00:07:36,900
bring oil to the surface. It's so combustible.

116
00:07:36,901 --> 00:07:40,000
And in the rush to develop, they spread oil all over

117
00:07:40,001 --> 00:07:42,460
everything, as far as you can see, getting that oil out as

118
00:07:42,461 --> 00:07:45,960
as fast as they can, setting the stage for serious fires anytime

119
00:07:46,060 --> 00:07:50,160
there's any kind of a spark.

120
00:07:50,260 --> 00:07:53,200
The invention of the automobile further fueled demand

121
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:56,500
for petroleum exploration.

122
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:59,520
Huge reserves discovered in places like Beaumont, Texas,

123
00:07:59,620 --> 00:08:06,160
in 1901 at Spindletop, ushered in the age of the gusher.

124
00:08:06,260 --> 00:08:09,020
A gusher occurs when drillers hit a greater than expected

125
00:08:09,021 --> 00:08:13,620
pocket of oil or gas, and oil shoots back up the wellbore

126
00:08:13,621 --> 00:08:19,460
uncontrolled. To add to the fire hazard,

127
00:08:19,461 --> 00:08:23,260
well owners initially stored oil in open pits before storage tanks

128
00:08:23,261 --> 00:08:27,080
were built. In the early fields, all the way

129
00:08:27,081 --> 00:08:32,180
into the 1950s, most of the fields burned, in part at least,

130
00:08:32,181 --> 00:08:35,320
when they were developed. It was just almost impossible to

131
00:08:35,321 --> 00:08:40,920
keep this from happening. But in 1922, Cameron

132
00:08:40,921 --> 00:08:47,220
Ironworks invented the first effective blowout preventer, which reduced blowouts and made drilling

133
00:08:47,221 --> 00:08:53,640
operations safer. Fewer blowouts meant fewer fires.

134
00:08:53,641 --> 00:09:00,400
Conversely, if a well wasn't producing as much as the owner thought it was capable of,

135
00:09:00,401 --> 00:09:05,680
a specialized workman called a torpedo shooter was called in.

136
00:09:05,681 --> 00:09:08,580
Torpedo shooters dropped explosive nitroglycerin

137
00:09:08,581 --> 00:09:14,380
canisters, called torpedoes, down the well to induce flow.

138
00:09:14,381 --> 00:09:17,460
They would literally drop that down the wellbore

139
00:09:17,461 --> 00:09:21,020
to fracture the rock down there or the sand

140
00:09:21,021 --> 00:09:24,120
so that it could produce more.

141
00:09:24,121 --> 00:09:27,020
That was an interesting group of men.

142
00:09:27,021 --> 00:09:34,680
I think it was once said that two out of three torpedo shooters died before they were 30 years old.

143
00:09:34,760 --> 00:09:36,720
It was a terribly dangerous line of work.

144
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:41,960
One of those daring oil field workers was a shooter named Carl Kinley.

145
00:09:43,940 --> 00:09:48,820
Carl was a widower who worked in the oil fields of Central California to support his three children.

146
00:09:48,821 --> 00:09:55,820
On April 29, 1913, Carr was helping clear a valve off a

147
00:09:55,821 --> 00:09:58,820
damaged oil rig from a small fire.

148
00:09:58,821 --> 00:10:02,820
Until this time, no one had found a very effective way of

149
00:10:02,821 --> 00:10:04,820
snuffing these fires.

150
00:10:04,821 --> 00:10:09,820
They felt like they may have a way of controlling the fire

151
00:10:09,821 --> 00:10:13,820
with steam if they were to get the fire going straight up.

152
00:10:13,821 --> 00:10:14,903
straight up.

153
00:10:14,904 --> 00:10:21,940
And so the procedure called for using explosives to blow a valve off the well.

154
00:10:23,740 --> 00:10:27,600
Carl loaded some nitroglycerin and carefully placed it by the rig.

155
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:33,560
But when he detonated the nitro, an amazing thing happened. The fire went out.

156
00:10:35,100 --> 00:10:40,620
Moments later, the oil reignited. No one thought much of the fire having gone out,

157
00:10:40,621 --> 00:10:46,560
except for Carl's oldest son, Myron, who was standing nearby.

158
00:10:46,620 --> 00:10:49,960
14-year-old Myron Kinley knew he had just witnessed something

159
00:10:50,020 --> 00:10:52,060
important.

160
00:10:52,140 --> 00:10:55,100
Little did he know, he'd seen the birth of a multi-million

161
00:10:55,160 --> 00:11:00,480
dollar industry he'd soon helped create.

162
00:11:00,540 --> 00:11:04,340
In the late 19th century, the main product of petroleum oil

163
00:11:04,400 --> 00:11:08,020
was kerosene, which competed with whale oil as the prime

164
00:11:08,021 --> 00:11:13,020
source of illumination. Oil Firefighting will return on

165
00:11:13,021 --> 00:11:20,020
Modern Marvels. We now return to Oil Firefighting

166
00:11:20,021 --> 00:11:27,020
on Modern Marvels. Myron Kinley, middle school dropout

167
00:11:27,021 --> 00:11:32,020
and oil patch shooters assistant, almost single-handedly created

168
00:11:32,021 --> 00:11:37,020
the modern oil firefighting industry.

169
00:11:37,021 --> 00:11:41,020
The concept simmered in his mind after he witnessed his father

170
00:11:41,021 --> 00:11:45,020
inadvertently blow out a well fire using explosives.

171
00:11:45,021 --> 00:11:51,020
But it didn't happen overnight. First, Myron saw action during the

172
00:11:51,021 --> 00:11:56,020
First World War in an artillery unit, gaining experience on how

173
00:11:56,021 --> 00:12:01,020
to set explosive charges. After the war, he joined his younger

174
00:12:01,021 --> 00:12:05,020
brother Floyd in a marginally successful torpedo shooting

175
00:12:05,021 --> 00:12:11,320
business, using explosives to stimulate well production.

176
00:12:11,321 --> 00:12:15,560
But one event would change the brothers' business plan.

177
00:12:15,561 --> 00:12:22,080
In 1924, Myron and Floyd watched as men struggled for 12 days to

178
00:12:22,081 --> 00:12:26,180
put out a burning well in Cromwell, Oklahoma, using water

179
00:12:26,181 --> 00:12:29,320
and dirt.

180
00:12:29,321 --> 00:12:34,120
Remembering Carl's example 11 years earlier, Myron offered to

181
00:12:34,121 --> 00:12:44,460
put the fire out in two days for the then huge sum of $500.

182
00:12:44,560 --> 00:12:47,560
Myron realized his father's earlier torpedo had sucked up

183
00:12:47,660 --> 00:12:53,980
all the available oxygen, suffocating the fire.

184
00:12:54,080 --> 00:12:57,280
If Myron could just build a big enough torpedo,

185
00:12:57,380 --> 00:12:59,580
he could blow out the Cromwell fire

186
00:12:59,680 --> 00:13:01,480
and prevent it from immediately reigniting.

187
00:13:04,120 --> 00:13:09,360
the well owner agreed to the deal without expecting the crazy notion to work.

188
00:13:09,361 --> 00:13:15,060
But Myron proved his theory with a terrific explosion.

189
00:13:15,061 --> 00:13:21,060
At that moment, he knew he'd created his new line of work.

190
00:13:21,061 --> 00:13:28,220
He soon quit torpedo shooting and became a full-time oil well firefighter.

191
00:13:28,221 --> 00:13:37,560
By 1931, his business had grown to a point that he moved from Tulsa to Houston, the center

192
00:13:37,561 --> 00:13:43,360
of the oil well service industry.

193
00:13:43,361 --> 00:13:47,980
Within a few years, Myron and Floyd Kinley were legendary.

194
00:13:47,981 --> 00:13:54,820
Oil men all over the world knew to call Kinley if they had a blowout.

195
00:13:54,821 --> 00:13:58,460
The Kinley brothers honed their technique for dousing and capping fires throughout the

196
00:13:58,461 --> 00:14:02,140
late 20s and 30s.

197
00:14:02,141 --> 00:14:08,360
When they worked together, it was a very businesslike atmosphere.

198
00:14:08,361 --> 00:14:12,860
And one person was in charge, whether it was my father or my uncle.

199
00:14:12,861 --> 00:14:21,440
They had the utmost respect for one another, and I think they cared deeply about one another.

200
00:14:21,441 --> 00:14:24,140
The Kinleys found that relatively lightweight sheets

201
00:14:24,141 --> 00:14:27,580
of corrugated galvanized tin deflected heat better

202
00:14:27,581 --> 00:14:28,580
than other materials.

203
00:14:31,880 --> 00:14:35,140
The Kinleys also used hoses to create a constant and drenching

204
00:14:35,141 --> 00:14:40,940
artificial rain, keeping men and equipment from incinerating.

205
00:14:40,941 --> 00:14:44,340
The water also lessened chances of a spark reigniting a snuffed

206
00:14:44,341 --> 00:14:46,740
well.

207
00:14:46,741 --> 00:14:49,840
For this reason, the site around a burning well was always raked

208
00:14:49,841 --> 00:14:56,080
clean of debris so that nothing hot could start another fire.

209
00:14:56,140 --> 00:14:58,380
Though the process was slow and tedious,

210
00:14:58,440 --> 00:15:01,680
it meant life or death for the Kinleys.

211
00:15:01,760 --> 00:15:03,820
I think you've got to clear away all the debris away from

212
00:15:03,880 --> 00:15:06,420
the wellhead and protect that wellhead at all times because

213
00:15:06,500 --> 00:15:09,460
that's what you want to work on.

214
00:15:09,520 --> 00:15:12,460
When it came time to actually blow out the well,

215
00:15:12,520 --> 00:15:17,260
Myram packed explosives into a shot canister.

216
00:15:17,261 --> 00:15:20,300
were inserted into the shot to detonate the load when they had

217
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:21,700
it where they wanted it.

218
00:15:24,740 --> 00:15:30,200
The canister was then delicately placed next to the burning fire.

219
00:15:30,300 --> 00:15:33,040
Initially, the brothers carried the explosives up to the mouth

220
00:15:33,140 --> 00:15:38,120
of the burning well, set it down under a constant stream of water,

221
00:15:38,220 --> 00:15:41,120
then ran like hell before the canister was detonated.

222
00:15:43,620 --> 00:15:45,680
Of course, the Kinley brothers were only half finished when

223
00:15:45,681 --> 00:15:48,080
finished when the fire was out.

224
00:15:48,180 --> 00:15:49,580
Most people don't understand.

225
00:15:49,680 --> 00:15:51,380
Getting rid of the fire is the easiest thing.

226
00:15:51,480 --> 00:15:52,580
Hell, you know.

227
00:15:52,680 --> 00:15:54,920
But you got to cap the well because it's still blowing.

228
00:15:55,020 --> 00:15:56,420
It just isn't on fire.

229
00:15:56,520 --> 00:15:59,220
And that's where the work comes in.

230
00:15:59,320 --> 00:16:02,440
Under a spray of volatile oil or gas,

231
00:16:02,540 --> 00:16:06,140
the Kinleys cut off the damaged well head.

232
00:16:06,240 --> 00:16:10,140
On this well, they used metal hacksaws.

233
00:16:10,240 --> 00:16:12,540
They had to be careful, as any spark

234
00:16:12,541 --> 00:16:16,540
the well, incinerating the men.

235
00:16:16,640 --> 00:16:20,080
The brothers then installed a new series of valves known as a

236
00:16:20,180 --> 00:16:24,080
Christmas tree. Finally, they shut the new Christmas

237
00:16:24,180 --> 00:16:31,000
tree, capping the gusher. Myron and Floyd were always

238
00:16:31,100 --> 00:16:36,100
careful, but sometimes the unexpected happened.

239
00:16:36,101 --> 00:16:41,100
In 1929, near Gladewater, Texas, Myron got hurt.

240
00:16:41,101 --> 00:16:46,100
He was hooking a cable onto debris.

241
00:16:46,101 --> 00:16:50,100
When his leg was trapped, he could have very easily burned up.

242
00:16:50,101 --> 00:16:53,100
Had his brother Floyd not been there very quickly

243
00:16:53,101 --> 00:16:57,100
to take him out of that situation, he saved his life.

244
00:16:59,100 --> 00:17:02,100
Myron was fortunate to escape with only a broken leg.

245
00:17:02,101 --> 00:17:09,100
The brothers discovered the hard way what worked and what didn't.

246
00:17:09,101 --> 00:17:14,100
They've had a monopoly, if you will, on the business of oil

247
00:17:14,101 --> 00:17:18,100
wellfire fighting all those years.

248
00:17:18,101 --> 00:17:21,100
But success had its price.

249
00:17:21,101 --> 00:17:25,100
In 1939, the dangers of the profession struck home for the

250
00:17:25,101 --> 00:17:27,100
Kindley family.

251
00:17:27,101 --> 00:17:31,100
Oh, his brother, Floyd, got killed in south Texas somewhere

252
00:17:31,101 --> 00:17:36,100
back off some drill pipe and it come loose and that's what hit him in the head, I think

253
00:17:36,101 --> 00:17:39,740
killed him. Myron was battling another fire,

254
00:17:39,741 --> 00:17:45,540
too far away to save Floyd, the way Floyd had saved him years earlier.

255
00:17:45,541 --> 00:17:52,950
It was a terrible tragedy, the whole family. Floyd and Myron were very close.

256
00:17:52,951 --> 00:17:53,720
It was a

257
00:17:53,721 --> 00:17:57,960
terrible thing. Myron's wife had the sad task

258
00:17:57,961 --> 00:18:01,760
of telling Floyd's wife the news.

259
00:18:01,860 --> 00:18:05,460
After dinner, I was sent out with someone to go to a movie

260
00:18:05,560 --> 00:18:08,300
so that Aunt Marguerite would be there.

261
00:18:08,400 --> 00:18:09,700
The house would be quiet.

262
00:18:09,800 --> 00:18:13,180
Mother took that way of telling Aunt Marguerite

263
00:18:13,280 --> 00:18:15,740
about the loss of her husband.

264
00:18:15,840 --> 00:18:20,820
Aunt Marguerite thought she was going to see him the next day,

265
00:18:20,920 --> 00:18:23,260
you know, go down there and all, and he's dead.

266
00:18:23,261 --> 00:18:28,260
With Floyd dead, Myron needed an experienced partner

267
00:18:28,360 --> 00:18:31,160
to help him with all the business coming his way.

268
00:18:31,260 --> 00:18:36,200
It seemed as if there was nobody else with the skills, bravery,

269
00:18:36,300 --> 00:18:39,100
and foolhardiness necessary.

270
00:18:39,200 --> 00:18:43,620
That is, until the greatest oil well firefighter who ever lived

271
00:18:43,720 --> 00:18:47,120
walked into Myron Kinley's office looking for a job.

272
00:18:47,121 --> 00:18:52,120
for a job. In 1931, Myron Kinley battled an

273
00:18:52,121 --> 00:18:57,120
oil well fire in Romania that had burned for more than two years.

274
00:18:57,121 --> 00:19:00,120
Myron capped the well within three months, capturing

275
00:19:00,121 --> 00:19:04,120
international attention. Oil firefighting will return on

276
00:19:04,121 --> 00:19:11,120
Modern Marvels. We now return to oil firefighting

277
00:19:11,121 --> 00:19:17,120
on Modern Marvels. Throughout the late 1930s,

278
00:19:17,121 --> 00:19:22,120
1940s, Myron Kinley continued to use his tried and true methods

279
00:19:22,220 --> 00:19:27,120
to put oil fires out with modest technological advances.

280
00:19:29,660 --> 00:19:33,120
The introduction of heavy-duty caterpillar tractors sped

281
00:19:33,220 --> 00:19:35,120
debris clearing considerably.

282
00:19:35,220 --> 00:19:40,120
In conjunction, Myron modified a logging trailer known as an

283
00:19:40,220 --> 00:19:43,120
Aethiwagon to suit his needs.

284
00:19:43,121 --> 00:19:47,200
The Athey wagon was important in the history of the profession

285
00:19:47,300 --> 00:19:50,500
because it allowed the well control experts

286
00:19:50,600 --> 00:19:54,460
to place the charge exactly where it needed to be placed.

287
00:19:54,560 --> 00:19:58,460
And an Athey wagon is simply a big, large boom that

288
00:19:58,560 --> 00:20:00,880
can be backed into the fire, and it

289
00:20:00,980 --> 00:20:04,700
can be raised and lowered and manipulated into exactly

290
00:20:04,800 --> 00:20:05,640
the right spot.

291
00:20:09,140 --> 00:20:13,120
Athey wagons could also drag away debris.

292
00:20:13,121 --> 00:20:15,920
It's a tool still used today.

293
00:20:16,020 --> 00:20:18,160
This wagon is ready for transport.

294
00:20:18,260 --> 00:20:21,060
You see the A-leg, we call it, on top.

295
00:20:21,160 --> 00:20:25,100
It basically folds up vertically when it's in use.

296
00:20:25,200 --> 00:20:27,400
We have a series of block and tackle

297
00:20:27,500 --> 00:20:32,000
that have a one-inch cable that goes to the drum or winch

298
00:20:32,100 --> 00:20:33,200
of a bulldozer.

299
00:20:33,300 --> 00:20:37,820
That conveys this boom up and down.

300
00:20:37,920 --> 00:20:40,840
Despite these advances, putting out oil well fires

301
00:20:40,841 --> 00:20:43,140
was still an extremely dangerous way to make a living

302
00:20:43,240 --> 00:20:46,240
in the late 1930s.

303
00:20:46,340 --> 00:20:50,840
No one knew this better than Myron Kinley.

304
00:20:50,940 --> 00:20:55,180
Myron was working on an oil well fire near Baytown, Texas.

305
00:20:55,280 --> 00:21:00,300
He was loading his shot canister full of explosives

306
00:21:00,400 --> 00:21:02,800
in preparation of being able to shoot the fire out,

307
00:21:02,900 --> 00:21:05,300
and the canister prematurely went off.

308
00:21:10,840 --> 00:21:15,580
Myron survived, but suffered a severe leg injury and walked

309
00:21:15,680 --> 00:21:19,340
with a limp for the rest of his life.

310
00:21:19,440 --> 00:21:23,980
Fortunately, in 1946, Myron Kindley discovered the best

311
00:21:24,080 --> 00:21:29,900
technology he'd ever have, a 5-foot, 6-inch Houston-born

312
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:35,240
redhead named Paul Red Adair.

313
00:21:35,340 --> 00:21:38,240
31-year-old Adair fought in Europe during the Second World

314
00:21:38,241 --> 00:21:41,740
World War with an explosives unit.

315
00:21:41,840 --> 00:21:43,080
We picked up a little more knowledge

316
00:21:43,180 --> 00:21:46,080
with different shaped charges and the schooling you went

317
00:21:46,180 --> 00:21:47,580
through and bombed the spot on them.

318
00:21:47,680 --> 00:21:51,080
So it helped me in fighting fires too.

319
00:21:51,180 --> 00:21:53,680
Red discovered he could actually shape a charge

320
00:21:53,780 --> 00:21:59,500
or direct the charge for a more efficient explosion or shot.

321
00:21:59,600 --> 00:22:05,000
In that drum, you've got, say, 250 pounds of dynamite.

322
00:22:05,100 --> 00:22:07,240
You need to put the caps in a proper place

323
00:22:07,241 --> 00:22:10,280
that the explosion will be directed in the direction

324
00:22:10,380 --> 00:22:11,780
that you want it to go in.

325
00:22:11,880 --> 00:22:14,180
You don't put the steel in the middle and just blow it up.

326
00:22:17,080 --> 00:22:18,980
Myron saw as they worked together

327
00:22:19,080 --> 00:22:23,660
that Red had an instinct, a talent for explosives.

328
00:22:23,760 --> 00:22:24,920
It's an art in handling an explosion

329
00:22:25,020 --> 00:22:26,420
a different way you have to use it.

330
00:22:32,060 --> 00:22:36,000
Working with Red in the shadow of a roaring, blowing well,

331
00:22:36,001 --> 00:22:40,000
devised a communication system based on hand signals.

332
00:22:40,100 --> 00:22:45,000
At first, Mr. Adair actually went on jobs with Myron,

333
00:22:45,100 --> 00:22:50,940
and then as more and more work came about, it was necessary for

334
00:22:51,040 --> 00:22:54,860
the two of them to split up occasionally, much like Myron

335
00:22:54,960 --> 00:22:58,960
did with his brother Floyd. Red Adair soon

336
00:22:59,060 --> 00:23:03,400
outshined the older, injury-wracked Myron Kinley.

337
00:23:03,401 --> 00:23:08,400
Andy began to develop his own style for putting out fires.

338
00:23:08,401 --> 00:23:12,400
The hellish nature of their occupation drew the two men

339
00:23:12,401 --> 00:23:15,400
together. He liked him a lot.

340
00:23:15,401 --> 00:23:18,400
There was a lot of camaraderie. There were jokes.

341
00:23:18,401 --> 00:23:23,400
There were good feelings between them, and there had to be a

342
00:23:23,401 --> 00:23:28,400
great deal of trust. I think Myron Kinley in many ways

343
00:23:28,401 --> 00:23:32,400
thought of Red Adair as his son, and the two of them were very

344
00:23:32,401 --> 00:23:36,300
very close. But Myron was unwilling to give up

345
00:23:36,301 --> 00:23:42,440
fighting fires entirely or give over the company to his protege.

346
00:23:42,441 --> 00:23:47,180
In 1951, as Myron curtailed his own schedule, Red hired Boots

347
00:23:47,181 --> 00:23:52,720
Hanson, a former field hand, to work as an assistant.

348
00:23:52,721 --> 00:23:56,320
I went with Mr. Kenley because he was crippling one thing

349
00:23:56,321 --> 00:23:58,160
or another. He'd been in it longer than Red.

350
00:23:58,161 --> 00:24:03,160
He had a way that he did a job.

351
00:24:03,260 --> 00:24:07,040
And when I worked for Red, I learned how to do it a different way.

352
00:24:07,140 --> 00:24:09,400
And a lot of times, it was a whole lot easier.

353
00:24:10,740 --> 00:24:14,940
In 1958, Coutts Matthews, a worker for the Halliburton Oil

354
00:24:15,040 --> 00:24:17,820
Field Services Company, joined the Kinley Company

355
00:24:17,920 --> 00:24:19,680
as another assistant.

356
00:24:19,780 --> 00:24:22,520
Butts and I were their helpers, just how it rotated.

357
00:24:22,620 --> 00:24:24,060
Sometimes he went out with Mr. Kinley,

358
00:24:24,160 --> 00:24:25,380
and sometimes he went out with Red.

359
00:24:25,480 --> 00:24:27,120
It's just how it felt, you know?

360
00:24:28,160 --> 00:24:32,600
Also in 1958, Myron attempted to put out a monster of a fire

361
00:24:32,700 --> 00:24:36,400
in Iran called Awaz.

362
00:24:36,500 --> 00:24:40,140
Despite all his experience and his best efforts,

363
00:24:40,240 --> 00:24:41,800
Myron couldn't kill the fire.

364
00:24:45,720 --> 00:24:50,180
While making a second attempt at blowing the fire out,

365
00:24:50,280 --> 00:24:53,820
he suffered a mild heart attack.

366
00:24:53,920 --> 00:24:56,980
Physically and emotionally exhausted,

367
00:24:56,981 --> 00:25:02,660
62-year-old Myron called for Red back in Texas for help.

368
00:25:02,680 --> 00:25:05,060
And he come at me and said, I think we met our match.

369
00:25:05,080 --> 00:25:06,560
I said, oh, no, we can get it.

370
00:25:06,580 --> 00:25:09,600
So we built up a big horseshoe shape charge.

371
00:25:09,620 --> 00:25:12,100
Red crafted a charge in the shape of a horseshoe

372
00:25:12,120 --> 00:25:13,800
to fit around the mouth of the well.

373
00:25:13,820 --> 00:25:16,660
He had five failures, then success.

374
00:25:20,600 --> 00:25:21,840
Boy, and he was happy.

375
00:25:21,880 --> 00:25:23,440
Got it this time.

376
00:25:23,480 --> 00:25:26,980
Boots and I capped it.

377
00:25:26,981 --> 00:25:31,020
Despite his heart attack in Iran, Myron was still not ready to

378
00:25:31,021 --> 00:25:32,020
retire.

379
00:25:32,021 --> 00:25:36,020
But Red Adair was ready to move on.

380
00:25:36,021 --> 00:25:41,020
In 1959, Red abruptly left the M.M. Kinley Company and formed

381
00:25:41,021 --> 00:25:46,020
his own oil firefighting company, taking Boots Hansen with him.

382
00:25:46,021 --> 00:25:50,020
Coots Matthews followed within a year.

383
00:25:50,021 --> 00:25:54,020
For the first time, the Kinley Company had real competition.

384
00:25:54,021 --> 00:25:57,820
Two legitimate oil firefighting companies now competed for

385
00:25:57,920 --> 00:25:59,360
business.

386
00:25:59,460 --> 00:26:02,620
I think my father was disappointed and hurt,

387
00:26:02,720 --> 00:26:07,960
perhaps, not so much that Red left him, left the company,

388
00:26:08,060 --> 00:26:09,960
because I think that was inevitable.

389
00:26:10,060 --> 00:26:13,140
But I think it was somehow something happened in the way

390
00:26:13,240 --> 00:26:17,080
it was done that hurt my father.

391
00:26:17,180 --> 00:26:18,520
I think he wished him well.

392
00:26:18,620 --> 00:26:24,020
And once it was over, I think it was all right with him.

393
00:26:24,021 --> 00:26:30,460
Myron Kinley, the man who almost single-handedly created the methods and techniques for fighting oil well fires,

394
00:26:31,420 --> 00:26:33,140
continued fighting fires on his own.

395
00:26:34,580 --> 00:26:38,540
He finally retired in 1962 at age 66.

396
00:26:40,520 --> 00:26:43,620
Myron spent his remaining years developing well service tools.

397
00:26:45,440 --> 00:26:49,880
Red Adair spent his time becoming the most famous oil well firefighter who ever lived,

398
00:26:49,881 --> 00:26:54,480
putting out the biggest oil well fires the world had ever seen.

399
00:26:57,560 --> 00:27:02,520
In 1936, Kilgore, Texas, was known as the world's richest acre,

400
00:27:03,060 --> 00:27:08,220
with 24 individual oil wells crowding a single block in the middle of town.

